1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:03,276
Three sections in, you've built vocabulary and a spec.

2
00:00:03,276 --> 00:00:06,310
None of it tells you whether an output is actually good.

3
00:00:06,310 --> 00:00:07,393
That's this section.

4
00:00:07,393 --> 00:00:11,185
If you only finish one part of this course properly, make it this one.

5
00:00:11,185 --> 00:00:15,411
Everything after it, RAG, agents, cost, risk, assumes you can already do this.

6
00:00:15,961 --> 00:00:18,496
Five ideas, and two things you actually build.

7
00:00:18,496 --> 00:00:23,125
You'll build a golden set, fifty examples chosen for what they teach you rather than

8
00:00:23,125 --> 00:00:24,502
how many you can collect.

9
00:00:24,502 --> 00:00:28,800
You'll write a rubric, then check whether five people using it agree with each

10
00:00:28,800 --> 00:00:29,131
other.

11
00:00:29,131 --> 00:00:33,429
You'll learn where an AI judge earns your trust, and where it quietly flatters

12
00:00:33,429 --> 00:00:33,815
itself.

13
00:00:33,815 --> 00:00:38,223
And you'll build two working eval harnesses: one in a spreadsheet, one in thirty

14
00:00:38,223 --> 00:00:42,411
lines of Python, so you own the number instead of asking an engineer for it.

15
00:00:42,411 --> 00:00:43,237
That's the arc.

16
00:00:43,237 --> 00:00:45,993
Let's start with the mindset underneath all of it.
